alice hindi says her brother josh was  the life and soul of the party  at 21 he had so much ahead of him but in  november  he took his own life i've got really  happy memories of of my brother  i just wish he was he was still here  with me souvenirs from holidays together  are among the items alice uses to  trigger those fond memories  but after discovering how josh was able  to research suicide on the internet  before his death  she was determined to do more than just  remember him creating an online tool  to act as a safety net for others in  need of help  my main motivation for this is to  prevent other  families from going through the pain  and the complete devastation that this  causes  and if i can just save one more person  from from this  you know happening to them then as far  as i'm concerned i would have done my  job  the software alice created is called  ripple it's designed to intervene when  someone searches for harmful  content by redirecting users to a page  where they can seek help from charities  instead  the samaritan's helpline does already  appear at the top of such  searches but alice says it's not enough  what i'm trying to do is provide  much more of an interception before the  users get to those search results  and provide a range of different mental  health support options  so not just the samaritan's helpline  number that currently exists  but also a text service a web chat  facility  the downloading of resources or an app  given the impact of the pandemic on  mental health charities agree  urgent action is needed we need sites to  have robust content policies in place  that govern the type of content allowed  with effective mechanisms to remove  content that breaks their policies  or at the very least makes it much much  harder to find harmful content  online content encouraging suicide is  already illegal  and the government told sky news that  platforms must do more to stop it  appearing  it's hoped the new online harms bill  will help make the internet  a safer place by giving platforms a  legal duty of care to protect their  users but while such legislation is  welcomed  some believe more still needs to be done  iva bennett  sky news
 

